rg )\nL156: \nL157: We gratefully acknowledge support from our major funders, cite43†member institutions†info.arxiv.org , , and all contributors.\nL158: cite44†About†info.arxiv.org · cite45†Help†info.arxiv.org · cite46†Contact†info.arxiv.org · cite47†Subscribe†info.arxiv.org · cite48†Copyright†info.arxiv.org · cite49†Privacy†info.arxiv.org · cite50†Accessibility†info.arxiv.org · cite51†Operational Status (opens in new tab)†status.arxiv.org L159: \nL160: Major funding support from\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n[2602.00521v1] Diagnosing the Reliability of LLM-as-a-Judge via Item Response Theory (https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.00521v1)\nciteturn25997view2 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: yesterday; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.00521v1\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 161\nL0: cite0†Search cite1†Submit cite2†Donate†info.arxiv.org cite3†Log in L1: \nL2: Search arXiv [Input: Search papers by title, author, abstract, or ID...] [Input] [Input]\nL3: \nL4: Press Enter to search · cite4†Advanced search L5: \nL6: # Computer Science > Artificial Intelligence\nL7: \nL8: [Submitted on 31 Jan 2026 (this version), latest version 29 May 2026 (cite5†v2 )]\nL9: # Title:Diagnosing the Reliability of LLM-as-a-Judge via Item Response Theory\nL10: \nL11: Authors:cite6†Junhyuk Choi , cite7†Sohhyung Park , cite8†Chanhee Cho , cite9†Hyeonchu Park , cite10†Bugeun Kim L12: \nL13: View a PDF of the paper titled Diagnosing the Reliability of LLM-as-a-Judge via Item Response Theory, by Junhyuk Choi and 3 other authors\nL14: \nL15: cite11†View PDF cite12†HTML (experimental) L16: > Abstract:While LLM-as-a-Judge is widely used in automated evaluation, existing validation practices primarily operate at the level of observed outputs, offering limited insight into whether LLM judges themselves function as stable and reliable measurement instruments. To address this limitation, we introduce a two-phase diagnostic framework for assessing reliability of LLM-as-a-Judge, grounded in Item Response Theory (IRT).\nL17: The framework adopts Graded Response Model (GRM) of IRT and formalizes reliability along two complementary dimensions: (1) intrinsic consistency, defined as the stability of measurement behavior under prompt variations, and (2) human alignment, capturing correspondence with human quality assessments. We empirically examine diverse LLM judges with this framework, and show that leveraging IRT-GRM yields interpretable signals for diagnosing judgments systematically.\nL18: These signals provide practical guidance for verifying reliablity of LLM-as-a-Judge and identifying potential causes of unreliability.\nL19: Comments:  | Under review\nL20: Subjects:  | Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)\nL21: Cite as:  | cite13†arXiv:2602.00521 [cs.AI]\nL22:    | (or cite14†arXiv:2602.00521v1 [cs.AI] for this version)\nL23:    | cite15†https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2602.00521†doi.org arXiv-issued DOI via DataCite\nL24: ## Submission history\nL25: \nL26: From: Junhyuk Choi [cite16†view email ]\nL27: [v1] Sat, 31 Jan 2026 05:24:08 UTC (143 KB)\nL28: cite5†[v2] Fri, 29 May 2026 10:53:54 UTC (150 KB)\nL29: \nL30: Full-text links:\nL31: \nL32: ## Access Paper:\nL33: \nL34: View a PDF of the paper titled Diagnosing the Reliability of LLM-as-a-Judge via Item Response Theory, by Junhyuk Choi and 3 other authors\nL35: \nL36:   * cite11†View PDF L37:   * cite12†HTML (experimental) L38:   * cite17†TeX Source L39: \nL40: cite18†view license†creativecommons.org L41: ### Current browse context:\nL42: \nL43: cs.AI\nL44: \nL45: cite19†< prev |   cite20†next > L46: \nL47: cite21†new | cite22†recent | cite23†2026-02 L48: \nL49: Change to browse by:\nL50: \nL51: cite24†cs L52: \nL53: ### References & Citations\nL54: \nL55:   * cite25†NASA ADS†ui.adsabs.harvard.edu L56:   * cite26†Google S